#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 19\ \ \ Energy\ and\ chance\ \ -\ \ a\ Hopfield\ network\ composes\ tone\ rows\,\ annealing\ helps\ (1983-85) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1100 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1100 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1100 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 144 neurons on a piano roll: one for every pitch at every position of a 12-note row. Rules (each pitch once \, one pitch per position) and a taste (some intervals cost more) become one ENERGY. The network rolls downhill - with noise that cools slowly \, it finds smooth rows., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 790 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ computing\ by\ lowering\ an\ energy 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Lesson 7 used the energy of a Hopfield network to store memories. Hopfield and Tank (1985) used it to SOLVE problems: write what you want as an energy \, turn it into weights \, and let the network fall., f 52;
#X text 20 203 THE PROBLEM: a twelve-tone row - each of the 12 pitches exactly once - whose neighbouring intervals sound good. It is the travelling-salesman problem: pitches are cities \, interval costs are distances \, and the row loops like a round trip., f 52;
#X text 20 299 THE ENERGY: E = A * (rule violations) + D * (taste cost). The rules are the n-rooks problem of Rojas 13.5: each row and each column of the roll holds exactly one unit. Every term becomes a weight: -2A between units in the same row or column \, -D * cost between neighbouring positions., f 52;
#X text 20 410 A HOPFIELD NETWORK (T = 0) only ever goes downhill and stops in the nearest valley: a valid row \, rarely a good one., f 52;
#X text 20 461 A BOLTZMANN MACHINE (Hinton & Sejnowski \, 1985) adds noise: a unit switches on with probability 1 / (1 + exp(-h / T)). Hot \, it jumps over the hills between valleys \, cooling slowly - simulated annealing (Kirkpatrick \, 1983) - it settles in deep ones. At a fixed T it visits every state with the Boltzmann probability exp(-E / T) / Z., f 52;
#X text 20 587 THE LIMIT: with the consonant taste the best row is the circle of fifths (cost 1.2). The network finds rows costing between 2 and 3 instead. Hopfield and Tank hoped for the best tour \, Wilson and Pawley (1988) and Bruck and Goodman showed it cannot be guaranteed (Rojas 13.5.6)., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 470 empty empty THE\ NETWORK\ \ (rows\ =\ pitches\,\ columns\ =\ positions\ in\ the\ row) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
