#X obj 10 896 cnv 1 1260 200 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 30 922 1) Raise the volume \, switch live on. At T = 0.3 the roll flickers and the row is full of clusters and gaps. Press anneal and listen: the noise cools \, units freeze \, a valid row emerges and keeps looping., f 56;
#X text 30 1003 2) Quench instead (T = 0.01 at once): it freezes into the nearest valley - a valid row \, but usually a rougher one (higher taste cost)., f 56;
#X text 446 922 3) Watch the energy graph during an anneal: it falls in steps \, with small jumps upwards - the noise that lets it escape shallow valleys., f 56;
#X text 446 973 4) Lower A (the rules) below 0.3: the taste wins \, positions get two pitches or none. Raise D: smoother rows \, more broken rules. The balance is the art of Hopfield-Tank networks., f 56;
#X text 862 922 5) Change the taste: steps gives near-chromatic lines \, thirds gives arpeggios. Whole-tone rows are impossible - two whole-tone scales never meet - so it must compromise., f 56;
#X text 862 988 6) Stay at T = 1 with live on: no row ever forms - the Boltzmann machine wanders through all states., f 56;
